/-!
# A two-level clock inside the common Hamiltonian of `Σ_*`: the pointer follows the clock's hand

**TERM-SCOPE(Hamiltonian)** — this module uses the *restricted* sense of "Hamiltonian";
`specs/TERMS.md` records what is backed and what is not.

**Category:** 7-SigmaLayer (the record layer). BACKLOG #357 — milestone **M4, part A** of the
author's programme "From `Σ` to physical spacetime". M4 asks that the clock be put inside the same
model as the detector: the clock's tick generated and recorded by the shared evolution, no
independently prescribed detector dynamics, and the recorded elapsed time related to the model's
relativistic clock law with its finite-splitting, localisation and detector-resolution errors.
This part builds the model and proves the law for the pointer's displacement, in the flow
parameter. It does not reach operational time: nothing here is a function of records alone.

**The model.** The carrier is the device of `RecordLayer/DiracTicks.lean`: one particle on the
Dirac ring of `M + 1` sites with a two-level internal mass, Hamiltonian `deviceHam J μ₀ μ₁`. The
arena is `ℂℙ^{4M+3} × T²`, the arena of #349 with the carrier's level doubled. The joint energy
`clockPointerEnergy`, an instance of `coupledEnergy`, is

  `𝓗(p, θ) = −2 ⟨H_dev⟩_p − 2 g f(θ₂) ⟨X⟩_p`,

with `X = levelSwap` the exchange of the two levels at every site (`clockPointerEnergy_eq`). Its
Hamiltonian flow `clockPointerFlow` has a closed form (`clockPointerFlow_apply`): `θ₂` is constant,
the device is moved by `exp (−i t (H_dev + g f(θ₂) X))`, and the pointer angle `θ₁` advances at the
rate `−2 g f'(θ₂) ⟨X⟩` (`hasDerivAt_clockPointerShift`). The pointer has no dynamics of its own:
its motion is the `θ₁`-component of the one flow.

**The law.** Along the device's uncoupled orbit `⟨X⟩` is twice the real part of `deviceCoherence`
(`clockFreeRead_deviceRay`), the coherence whose phase the free-chain clock theorems turn at the
level splitting `R` (`deviceCoherence_single`, `dirac_device`). So the pointer integrates the
clock's quadrature, and its displacement follows the hand
`clockHand R C₀ t = ∫₀ᵗ Re (e^{−iRs} C₀) ds`:

  `|Δθ₁(t) + 4 g f'(θ₂) clockHand R C₀ t / ‖Ψ₀‖²| ≤ 2 |f'(θ₂)| (g² |f(θ₂)| + |g| ε / ‖Ψ₀‖²) t²`

(`abs_clockPointerShift_sub_hand_le`). The term `g² |f(θ₂)|` is the back-reaction of the pointer's
static term on the device. The term `ε` is the drift of the device's coherence, its localisation
error: `0` for one mode (`clockFreeRead_single`) and `c D` for the packet of `dirac_device`
(`clock_pointer_device`). The rate `R` is the dilated one: `R/(μ₁ − μ₀)` is within `(μ₁ − μ₀)/E₀`
of `√(1 − (v/c)²)`, the finite-splitting error.

**What the pointer is.** The hand is periodic and bounded (`clockHand_add_period`,
`abs_clockHand_le`). A pointer driven by the unrectified quadrature swings and returns
(`clockPointerShift_single_add_period`); it does not count. Its turning points are the flow times
at which the coherence has turned to an odd multiple of `π/2`, `π/R` apart
(`clockRead_single_eq_zero_iff`). They are generated by the joint flow, and no schedule is
supplied. They are not yet records.

## The coupled flow in a carrier's coordinates

The pattern of #349 and #351 (`diracCarrierU`, `diracPointerShift`), for any Hermitian pair
`A₀`, `B₀` and any index equivalence. The Dirac ring was the first use and the clock is the second.

* `idxCoupledH`, `idxCoupledH_isHermitian`, `coupledMatrix_reindex`, `idxCoupledGen_skew`,
  `idxFreeGen_skew`;
* `idxCarrierU`, `idxFreeU`, `idxCarrierU_val`, `idxFreeU_val`, `hasDerivAt_idxCarrierU`,
  `continuous_idxCarrierU`, `continuous_idxFreeU`, `idxCarrierU_of_lift_eq_zero`,
  `idxUnitary_idxCarrierU`, `idxArenaEquiv_kick`;
* `schrodingerHamiltonian_reindex`, `coupledEnergy_reindex`;
* `idxPointerShift`, `idxPointerShift_zero`, `coupledAccum_reindex`, `idxPointerShift_eq`,
